# Reverse proxy in front of the stack: one origin for the browser, so the
# frontend can call the API with relative URLs (no CORS, no baked-in host).
#
# `{$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS::80}` is Caddy's environment-variable placeholder with
# a default value — the second colon starts the default, which is `:80`. That
# default serves plain HTTP on port 80 with no automatic TLS, which is what
# local `docker compose up` wants. Deployments set CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS to a
# hostname (e.g. arc.example.com) and Caddy obtains and renews a certificate
# on its own, serving HTTPS on 443.
#
# The /mcp route reaches the MCP server, which authenticates every request
# with a bearer key from MCP__API_KEYS — unauthenticated calls get a 401 from
# the MCP server itself, not from Caddy.
#
# `/sw.js` is the service worker (frontend/public/sw.js). It is served from
# the origin root because a worker controls only paths under its own URL, and
# it must never sit in an HTTP cache: a cached worker would keep a phone on the
# previous build until that cache expired. Next sets the same two headers
# (frontend/next.config.ts); Caddy sets them again after the proxy answers
# (`header_down`), so a change on either side cannot silently drop them.
#
# `request_body max_size` is the first bound on an upload: the API refuses
# anything over 100 MB (MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES) from the Content-Length header, but
# only after the request has reached it. This closes the connection at the
# edge instead, so a body nobody will accept is never spooled to the API
# container's disk. The 128 MB is deliberately above the application's limit:
# the 422 explaining the real rule should be what an athlete with a slightly
# too-large file sees, not a bare connection reset.
{$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS::80} {
	request_body {
		max_size 128MB
	}

	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}

	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}

	handle /mcp* {
		reverse_proxy mcp:8001
	}

	handle /sw.js {
		reverse_proxy frontend:3000 {
			header_down Cache-Control "no-cache"
			header_down Service-Worker-Allowed "/"
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy frontend:3000
	}
}

# Port 80, any host this site does not name. With CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS set to a
# hostname, Caddy answers port 80 for EVERY host with a 308 to https://, and the
# handshake then dies for names it holds no certificate for — so
# `http://localhost` against an `arc.localhost` deployment looked like a broken
# application. This site is more specific than that redirect only for hosts the
# real site does not claim (the real host keeps its 308), and it answers them
# with a sentence naming what IS served.
#
# 200, not 421: a browser shows a 200 body plainly and replaces a 4xx with its
# own error page, which would hide the one line that explains the mistake.
# Static `respond` only — an unserved hostname must never reach a backend.
#
# Inert by construction when CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS is the default `:80`: that site
# already answers every host on port 80 and wins (verified against Caddy 2.11,
# including `:80` and `http://arc.localhost`). There is no port-443 twin: with
# no certificate for the unmatched name there is no handshake to answer inside,
# so this plain-HTTP answer is the only reachable surface.
http:// {
	header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
	respond "arc does not serve this hostname. It is served at {$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS::80} - open that address instead (a bare hostname is https://)." 200
}
